// Account defines model for Account.
type Account struct {
	CreatedAt time.Time `json:"createdAt"`

	// CredentialKind **Every verifier stored for this identity, named by its scheme**, and
	// computed from whether a row exists in each opt-in table rather than
	// from a flag. A flag can be wrong; a `LEFT JOIN` cannot.
	//
	// `argon2id` is the only kind a portal or admin account may have, and it
	// is the verifier the portal and the console always authenticate against.
	//
	// `argon2id+nt` additionally stores an NT hash in a separate,
	// envelope-encrypted table, and exists solely for identities explicitly
	// opted in to PEAP-MSCHAPv2. An NT hash is password-equivalent — it is the
	// sole input to the MSCHAPv2 response, so a stolen hash authenticates
	// directly. The UI states this at the point of opt-in.
	//
	// `argon2id+bcrypt` additionally stores a **bcrypt** verifier, for an
	// identity explicitly opted in to **network authentication by password**
	// (RADIUS PAP from a NAS today; EAP-TTLS/PAP is planned). The scheme
	// is named here because **naming it is the price**: `rlm_pap` in FreeRADIUS 3.2.10
	// has no argon2 scheme and libxcrypt answers its failure token for
	// `$argon2id$` — measured — so an identity that authenticates to the
	// network over 802.1X does so against a verifier that is **not
	// argon2id**. A console that renders this as "argon2" is telling an
	// operator something the data plane cannot do.
	//
	// The two opt-ins are independent, so `argon2id+nt+bcrypt` is
	// representable. It is not reachable through this API today —
	// `internal/raddb` refuses by name to generate PEAP-MSCHAPv2 — and it is
	// in the enum because the schema can hold it and an enum that cannot
	// express a state the database can is a client that crashes on real data.
	//
	// Unlike the NT hash, a bcrypt verifier is **one-way**: reading it does
	// not authenticate. That is the entire reason it may live somewhere
	// `rlm_sql` can read, and the reason NAS PAP is served from it — and
	// EAP-TTLS/PAP is planned on it — where PEAP-MSCHAPv2 is refused.
	CredentialKind AccountCredentialKind `json:"credentialKind"`
	DisplayName    *string               `json:"displayName,omitempty"`
	Enabled        bool                  `json:"enabled"`
	Id             string                `json:"id"`
	LastLoginAt    *time.Time            `json:"lastLoginAt,omitempty"`

	// Plan What a guest is granted. Not what they have used.
	Plan     *Plan  `json:"plan,omitempty"`
	SiteId   string `json:"siteId"`
	Username string `json:"username"`
}

