// AuditEntry One recorded change. Read-only, forever — see `listAudit`.
type AuditEntry struct {
	ActorId string `json:"actorId"`

	// ActorUsername **Denormalised on purpose, and this is the member that makes the log
	// survivable.** An entry has to outlive the operator it names — people
	// leave, and their accounts are deleted — and a log that renders *"deleted
	// operator"* where the name should be is a log that fails exactly when it is
	// being read for a reason. Joining to `operators` at read time is the
	// obvious implementation and the wrong one.
	//
	// `actorId` is kept beside it so filtering by actor still works across a
	// username that was reused after a deletion.
	ActorUsername string    `json:"actorUsername"`
	At            time.Time `json:"at"`

	// Detail Optional fallback. Same rule as `title`.
	Detail *string `json:"detail,omitempty"`
	Id     string  `json:"id"`

	// Kind **The change, not a sentence about it.** The client renders the copy from its
	// own catalogue; the server sends the kind, the subject and the params.
	//
	// The list is what cannot be undone or what is security-relevant. It is closed,
	// and every value is produced by driving `web/mock` — the same rule
	// `ProblemType` carries, for the same reason: a kind nothing emits is a
	// catalogue entry that can never be shown to render correctly.
	//
	// **Operator sign-in and sign-out are deliberately not here.** They are events
	// rather than changes, they arrive at a rate nothing else in this list does, and
	// a log that is mostly sign-ins is a log in which the shared-secret rotation is
	// hard to find. What would add them is a place to put them — a separate
	// authentication-events surface, which `listAuthAttempts` already is for guests
	// and is not yet for operators.
	//
	// **Several members exist because, without them, a server would have nothing
	// legal to write and would leave the change unrecorded rather than
	// mislabelled** — the right call, and a hole in the log all the same:
	//
	// - `controller-updated` — `updateController` replaces the stored credential,
	//   the base URL or the TLS mode. That is the most security-relevant change
	//   this surface can make: a controller whose API key was swapped is a
	//   controller somebody else may now be driving. It is neither a registration
	//   nor a deletion.
	// - `controller-discovery-run` — one discovery reconciliation, whose subject is
	//   the **controller** and not any one device. `nas-registered` was the only
	//   near-enough value and it is the wrong one twice over: it would claim one
	//   entry for what may be nine adoptions, and it would name a device as the
	//   subject of a change made to a controller. Its `params` carry the counts
	//   (`adopted`, `updated`, `missing`) so the entry says what the run did.
	// - `site-created` — `createSite`, **the oldest entry any real deployment
	//   has**. A venue's name is the one fact about a deployment only the operator
	//   has, and a log that records every later change to it must also say where
	//   it came from. `nas-registered` is the near-enough value this rule refuses —
	//   a *device* as the subject of a change that made a *venue*.
	// - `session-disconnected` — an operator asked a device to end a guest's
	//   session. The subject is the **session** (`name` is the client MAC, the
	//   identifier the operator acted on), and it is recorded whether or not the
	//   device confirmed, because `params` also records what was withdrawn:
	//   `disconnected`, `grantsRevoked`, `voucherRevoked`, `voucherId`, `nasId`.
	//   `voucher-revoked` is not written beside it — one operator action is one
	//   entry, and its `params` say that a voucher went with it.
	Kind AuditKind `json:"kind"`

	// Params Structured values the client interpolates into its own copy — the number
	// of entries removed, the fields that changed, the reachability of the
	// device. Never a sentence.
	Params *map[string]interface{} `json:"params,omitempty"`

	// SiteId Null for a change that is not a site's — an operator was created, and
	// operators are deployment-wide. Not every recorded change belongs to a
	// site, and forcing one would be inventing a fact.
	//
	// **Which of the two a change is follows from its `kind`, not from the
	// operator's current venue**, because a null that answered both questions
	// at once would leave a reader unable to tell *this change had no venue*
	// from *nobody recorded the venue*. Reading it per kind is what makes the
	// null meaningful:
	//
	// - **The deployment's**, always null: `controller-registered`,
	//   `controller-updated`, `controller-deleted`, `operator-created`,
	//   `operator-updated`, `operator-deleted`, `operator-password-changed`,
	//   `retention-policy-changed`, `data-subject-erased`.
	//   A controller's vendor-site binding is per controller, so one can feed
	//   several venues and naming one would be the invented fact above; and
	//   there is no per-site operator role in this product.
	// - **A venue's**, always a site: every other kind, `site-created`
	//   included — the venue it created — and `controller-discovery-run`,
	//   whose request names the venue its adoptions land in.
	//
	// A client must not derive a filter from this: `listAudit` takes no
	// `siteId` parameter and deliberately takes none, because a good half of
	// the entries have none and filtering the screen by the header's site
	// switcher would hide exactly the ones an administrator opens it for. The
	// member is a **column**, and the console renders it as one.
	SiteId *string `json:"siteId,omitempty"`

	// Subject What the entry is about, named well enough to render after the thing itself is
	// gone. `name` is denormalised for the same reason `actorUsername` is: the
	// access point whose secret was rotated in March may not exist in June, and the
	// entry still has to say which one it was.
	Subject *AuditSubject `json:"subject,omitempty"`

	// Title **Optional fallback, for a `kind` an older console does not recognise**,
	// and the same rule as `Alarm.title`: a client that has copy for the kind
	// must ignore this. Two sources for one sentence is how they drift. `web/mock`
	// is asserted to send neither this nor `detail`.
	Title *string `json:"title,omitempty"`
}

