// CoaVendor **Which member of the CoA family a `driver: coa` device is.**
//
// `NasDriverKind` has one member for two vendors, and they are two different
// implementations because they identify a session differently: Cisco IOS-XE by
// a `Cisco-AVPair audit-session-id` VSA, Aruba by `Calling-Station-Id` in its
// own spelling. A MAC in the wrong spelling identifies no session — which a
// careful NAS answers with `Error-Cause 503` and a careless one answers by
// **ignoring the request and ACKing it**. `docs/rules/testing.md`: *"ACKing
// a CoA is trivial; faithfully rejecting one is not."* So the lab cannot catch
// a wrong guess, and nothing else in the product can either.
//
// **This is a separate field rather than two more members of `NasDriverKind`,
// and the reason is the one case that matters.** Splitting the enum into
// `cisco` and `aruba` would leave *nobody said* with nowhere to live — every
// row already stored as `coa` would have to be rewritten to one vendor or the
// other by a migration that has no way to know which, which is precisely the
// guess this field exists to prevent. A nullable field beside `driver: coa`
// keeps **unset** representable, so the server can **refuse** it by name
// instead of defaulting to a vendor and reporting success on a no-op.
//
// Nothing here is measured. No Cisco or Aruba device has been near this
// project; `internal/nas` records every capability of both as UNVERIFIED and
// only the wire encodings as DOCUMENTED, read from FreeRADIUS's own
// dictionaries. This field decides **which** unverified driver is built, never
// that it works.
type CoaVendor string

