// Failure A failure reported **inside a successful response**, rather than as one.
//
// `revokeVoucher` is the case that forced it: revoking a voucher succeeds, and
// ending the session it was being used for is a separate driver call that can
// fail on its own. The operation did what it was asked; something it attempted
// afterwards did not. That is a `200` carrying a failure, not a `4xx`.
//
// Two shapes were possible and this is the narrower one. Embedding a whole
// `Problem` would have meant inventing a `status` for a thing that has no
// status — writing `200` into a problem document is false, and writing `502` in
// the body of a `200` is worse. So this is `Problem`'s discriminating core with
// the HTTP-shaped members removed, keyed on **the same `ProblemType` enum**, so
// there is one catalogue and one keyspace rather than two that drift.
type Failure struct {
	// Detail Fallback only, for a `type` the client does not recognise. Same rule as
	// `Problem.detail`, and `web/mock` is asserted not to send it.
	Detail *string `json:"detail,omitempty"`

	// Params Structured values the client interpolates into its own copy.
	Params *map[string]interface{} `json:"params,omitempty"`

	// Type **The failure, not a sentence about it.** The client renders the copy from
	// its own catalogue; the server sends the type and its params.
	//
	// This is `AlarmKind`'s decision applied to refusals, using the mechanism
	// RFC 9457 already provides rather than inventing a parallel field. It is an
	// enum rather than an open string because a client catalogue keyed on an open
	// string is a `Record<string, Copy>`, which **proves nothing**: a missing entry
	// is a runtime hole. Keyed on this enum it is a `Record<ProblemType, Copy>` and a
	// missing entry is a compile error, exactly as it is for `AlarmKind`.
	//
	// `uri-reference`, not `uri`, and the difference is load-bearing rather than
	// pedantic: these are **identifiers, not addresses**. RFC 9457 permits a
	// relative reference and nothing dereferences them, so `/problems/validation`
	// is the honest spelling — `format: uri` rejects it, and every refusal the
	// validating mock sends would then fail validation and answer 500.
	//
	// ── what may and may not be added here ────────────────────────────────
	//
	// **Every value must be reachable by driving the server.** A value nothing can
	// produce is a catalogue entry that can never be shown to be right — a check
	// that only looks like one. `web/mock` drives one request per value and computes the expected set from
	// this enum, so adding a value here fails the mock suite until something can
	// emit it.
	//
	// **`/problems/validation` is deliberately not split**, even though it is the
	// most frequent of them. The discrimination it needs is per *field*, not per
	// request: the same request refuses `venueName` for being blank and `value` for
	// not being a hostname. So the page-level entry stays generic and the
	// field-level catalogue is keyed on `(type, field)` — see `FieldFault`, which
	// is the third part of that key where one field has more than one way to fail.
	//
	// **`PrintSetTooLarge` is deliberately absent, and this is where that is
	// recorded** so it is not rediscovered as an omission. The admin console's
	// batch-print loop has a bound of 100 pages of 200 vouchers; hitting it throws
	// a client-only `Error` subclass (`admin/src/screens/Vouchers.tsx`). It is not
	// in this enum for two reasons, and the second is the decisive one:
	//
	//   - **No server participates in it.** Every one of those 100 requests
	//     answered `200`. There is nothing for a server to send, so nothing here
	//     would describe a wire value.
	//   - **It could never be proven.** The condition needs 20 000 unused vouchers
	//     in one batch and the create dialog caps a batch at 2 000, so no drive
	//     against the mock can reach it — the one property every other value in
	//     this enum has. A value that cannot be driven is the vacuous catalogue
	//     entry this enum exists to prevent.
	//
	//   The client owns that sentence already and no contract is needed for it. If
	//   the contract ever gains a *print the whole batch* operation, its refusal
	//   would be server-sent and would earn a value here on that day.
	Type ProblemType `json:"type"`
}

// FieldFault **Why one field was refused** — the third part of the `(type, field)` key,
// and it exists because that pair is not unique.
//
// Measured, not assumed: `/problems/validation` refuses `value` on the walled
// garden three different ways (blank, not a hostname, not a CIDR range) and
// `username` on an account two (blank, taken). A catalogue keyed on
// `(type, field)` alone would silently render one of those sentences for all
// three, which is the same class of wrong answer as rendering the server's
// Vietnamese: confidently phrased and about a different failure.
//
// Carried in `Problem.params.fields`, keyed by exactly the same field names as
// `errors`. `errors` keeps the *prose*, which stays the fallback for a pair
// this client does not know; this carries the *kind*, which is what a client
// with copy renders from.
//
// **The two key sets are the same set.** A fault with no `errors` entry is a
// field the client cannot mark; an `errors` entry with no fault is a field the
// client can only describe in the server's language. `web/mock` is asserted on
// the equality rather than on either half, because either half alone passes for
// a server that has drifted.
type FieldFault string

// Freshness **The most important object in this document.** Any counter derived from
// RADIUS accounting travels with one of these, and the UI is not permitted to
// render the number without also rendering the freshness.
//
// Byte and time counters advance only on Interim-Update or Stop.
// `Acct-Interim-Interval` has a widely-implemented 300-second floor, so
// best-case sampling is five minutes — a worst-case overshoot of roughly
// 3.7 GB on a 100 Mbps link. Many NAS send no interim updates unless asked,
// and on the controller-API path there is no Access-Accept in which to ask.
type Freshness struct {
	// IntervalSeconds The requested `Acct-Interim-Interval`. Rendered next to the number.
	IntervalSeconds *int       `json:"intervalSeconds,omitempty"`
	SampledAt       *time.Time `json:"sampledAt,omitempty"`

	// State `live` — the value is authoritative right now (NAS-enforced, not sampled).
	// `sampled` — last updated within the requested interval.
	// `stale` — no update within 2× the requested interval. A fault.
	// `absent` — this NAS sends no accounting at all. **Never means "unlimited".**
	State FreshnessState `json:"state"`
}

