// Metric A figure and how old it is — and **the freshness is derived over exactly the
// rows the value counts.**
//
// That sentence is the whole of it, and it is written here because two servers
// each restating it in their own language will disagree. A server that counted
// `online` sessions and `orphaned` ones as two separate figures and took
// **one** sample time over both would report a venue whose guests had all been
// heard from thirty seconds ago as forty days stale, on the strength of a
// single abandoned session that figure does not contain. The number would be
// seconds old and the sentence beside it — which a client is required to
// render — forty days wrong.
//
// A figure whose rows are sampled from accounting therefore answers:
//
//  1. **Nothing counted → `live`, dated now.** There is no sample behind a
//     figure with no rows in it, and zero is what the server knows at that
//     instant. Never `sampled` with a null `sampledAt`, which names a sample
//     and then declines to date it.
//  2. **Otherwise `sampledAt` is the oldest of the counted rows**, never the
//     newest. A count is only as current as its least current row, and
//     reporting the freshest flatters it into looking live.
//  3. **`stale` when that sample is older than twice `intervalSeconds`**, which
//     for a counted figure is the 300-second `Acct-Interim-Interval` floor
//     rather than any one NAS's request — so the verdict is reachable by a
//     reader from the two numbers rendered beside it.
//
// A NAS that asked for a shorter interval and is late against its own promise
// is a *per-session* fault. It travels on that session's own `Freshness`,
// where there is deliberately no floor, and it is not what these three clauses
// measure.
type Metric struct {
	// Freshness **The most important object in this document.** Any counter derived from
	// RADIUS accounting travels with one of these, and the UI is not permitted to
	// render the number without also rendering the freshness.
	//
	// Byte and time counters advance only on Interim-Update or Stop.
	// `Acct-Interim-Interval` has a widely-implemented 300-second floor, so
	// best-case sampling is five minutes — a worst-case overshoot of roughly
	// 3.7 GB on a 100 Mbps link. Many NAS send no interim updates unless asked,
	// and on the controller-API path there is no Access-Accept in which to ask.
	Freshness Freshness `json:"freshness"`
	Value     *float32  `json:"value"`
}

