// PortalDesign **What a venue says to its guests, as opposed to how they sign in.** The
// settings above answer *who is this and how do I get on*; this answers *what
// does the venue want a guest to read* — the welcome line, today's offer, the
// menu, the breakfast hours, the Facebook page — and it is edited by the
// person who runs the café, not by whoever installed the network.
//
// **Blocks, not HTML.** Every captive-portal product that lets a non-technical
// owner build a page (Meraki, UniFi, Purple, Social WiFi, Spotipo) gives them
// a fixed set of content blocks rather than markup, and the reason here is
// sharper than ease of use: this page is served *before authentication* to
// anyone within radio range, so venue-authored markup would be stored XSS on a
// page a guest has just been asked to trust. Every string below is text, and
// every renderer escapes it.
//
// **Links only after sign-in.** Before the guest is authorised the only
// origin their phone can reach is the portal's own, so a link to Facebook on
// the welcome page is a button that spins and fails — the same reason there
// is no `terms.url` (see `PortalContext.terms`). `button` and `social`
// blocks are therefore refused anywhere but `connected`, with
