// SiteCreate **`name` is the venue's name and there is no default for it.** That is the
// reason `createSite` exists at all rather than a migration seeding a row: the
// one fact needed to make the first site is the one fact only the operator has.
//
// `id` is not accepted. A site id reaches `radius_clients`, `access_points`,
// `portal_settings`, `walled_garden_entries`, `vouchers` and every `audit_log`
// row, and an operator-chosen one is a permanent identifier typed once into a
// first-run form. The readable fixture ids (`site-hcm`, `site-lab`) are fixtures.
//
// **`environment` is not accepted either, and that is the narrow reading rather
// than an omission.** What this operation exists for is the *first* site, and a first
// site is a venue: `live`, which is what `sites.environment` already defaults to.
// `lab` marks a site an operator may point at fake gear; there is no
// `updateSite`, no console screen that sets it, and `docs/rules/frontend.md`
// is explicit that designing for a screen which does not exist is where wasted
// frontend work concentrates. Adding it later is a backward-compatible optional
// field — removing a value the console had been sending would not be.
//
// It also has no honest refusal today. A value outside the two members would
// need a `FieldFault`, and the closest existing one renders as *"Required."*
// beside a filled-in select — exactly the defect `fault.coaVendor` exists to
// prevent. A field whose only wrong answer produces a misleading sentence is a field
// to leave out until it has a screen and a fault of its own.
type SiteCreate struct {
	Name string `json:"name"`
}

