// NotFound RFC 9457.
//
// **`type` is the discriminator, and a client with copy for it must prefer its
// own.** The server cannot know the reader's language when it refuses a
// request, so `title` and `detail` are the *server's* wording and a fallback
// for a `type` the client does not recognise. Composing them in the server's
// language and rendering them verbatim would show Vietnamese to an
// English-speaking operator.
//
// **`params` is what makes that possible rather than merely required.** Without
// it a refusal that needs a number — *nine access points came from this
// controller*, *this is 140 kB against a 100 kB limit* — has nowhere to put the
// number except `detail`, which is the field the client must not render. So the
// client would reach for `detail` on precisely the refusals an operator most
// needs to understand, and the fallback would quietly become the real path.
// `Alarm.params` exists for the same reason and settled the same argument.
//
// **`title` stays required, and `detail` does not survive a server that knows
// its client.** They are not the same kind of thing: `title` is a short label
// and the only thing a console meeting a `type` it predates can render, so it
// is the fallback of last resort; `detail` is a composed sentence and is
// therefore where the drift and the wrong language live. A conforming server
// sends `detail` only for a `type` it expects clients not to know. `web/mock`
// is asserted to send none at all, with one named exception that is not a
// product answer — see the guard in `web/mock/src/server.test.ts`.
